Finding 1 (fixable, blocking the plan as written) — the plan never accounts for `-n auto` multiplying against #63's `tox run-parallel`, and its stated mitigation is contradicted by measurement. `tox run-parallel` runs all four envs concurrently; with `-n auto` each env claims every core, so a 16-core host runs ~64 pytest workers. Measured on this host, four concurrent envs: serial-within-env 53s wall (355 passed on each of py311/py312/py313/py314); `-n auto` within each env 67s wall (355 passed on each). The documented end-of-work command therefore gets ~26% slower, while the single-env command improves (Plan PoC: 36.57s serial to 26.65s at `-n auto`). Nothing green breaks, but two load-bearing plan statements are wrong for the matrix case: the Challenge entry treats over-wide `-n auto` as recoverable "via posargs", and the Technology Validation section forbids `--maxprocesses` on the grounds that "16-wide was still faster than serial here" — true for one env, false for four. Step 3 of the plan also sets no acceptance criterion beyond green, so this regression would land unrecorded. The plan must state the accepted trade-off before build and pick one of: accept and document the matrix cost, cap workers, or change the documented full-matrix invocation.

Finding 2 (fixable) — Acceptance Criterion 3 has no implementation step. The brief requires the serial-marker count be "justified in the PR or issue comment"; the count is zero, which is the interesting answer, but no plan unit produces that artifact and no Level 2 phase owns PR or issue communication (`level2-*.md`, `niko-reflect`, and `niko-archive` contain no PR or issue-comment steps). Add a step, or explicitly reassign the criterion to the operator.

Finding 3 (advisory) — mild test redundancy. `test_tox_commands_enable_xdist` re-asserts `{posargs}` presence, already covered by `test_tox_commands_run_pytest_with_posargs`, and `test_tox_deps_include_pytest_xdist` guards a condition the suite cannot survive anyway (with `-n auto` in commands and the plugin absent, pytest exits on an unrecognized argument). Both are consistent with existing precedent in this file and are harmless; no change requested.

